{
  "case_id": "2018-18-266",
  "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:09 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Is pain and suffering compensatory damages?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: It's not pecuniary?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Is all that right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "proximate cause of the injury. Under this Court's decision in CSX versus McBride, it is a more relaxed causation standard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0002-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The last difference that I would point to is that, for an unseaworthiness claim, you can get a maritime lien and attach the vessel. And that is an ancient remedy that is designed to ensure protection of the ward of the admiralty. You cannot attach the vessel in a Jones Act claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: But can we go back?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Frederick",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Say you've got a cruise ship that's owned by a particular cruise line, but they subcontract out to a Broadway company to have a traveling music show on the cruise ship. Now, under the Jones Act, it's the Broadway company that is the employer of the musician, but if the unseaworthiness condition causes an injury to that worker, that worker has a suit against the owner of the vessel for the unseaworthiness causing the condition but not a Jones Act remedy against his or her employer for that particular injury."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: So how do you think, Mr. Frederick, we should think about the question of the relationship between the Jones Act, on the one hand, and the common law maritime function, on the other?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Waxman",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The evidence -- with respect, Justice Ginsburg, the evidence of punitive damages in unseaworthiness cases, with all due respect, is not slim. It is utterly nonexistent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6da196a0b273-0002-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "damages were not available for a violation of the general maritime law of unseaworthiness."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2018-18-266",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "6da196a0b2731ee6f622e8d26b5453350586de4b341348f95d9381a1ae594bb8",
